# NOTICE

`dsh-compaction-jev` is an independent DeepSeek Harness plugin. It ports the
decision algorithm of a separately published project and reuses a DeepSeek
Harness package as its base. Both are recorded here.

## Ported from `fast-jev-compaction`

The decision plan in `src/jev/` and the checkpoint rendering in `src/surface.ts`
are a TypeScript port of **`fast-jev-compaction`** v0.2.0
(<https://github.com/tamaratran/fast-jev-compaction>), which is distributed
under the MIT License, Copyright (c) 2025. That license text is reproduced
verbatim under "Third-party license text" below.

The upstream project is a Claude Code plugin; its decision core is reached only
through Claude Code hook entry points. That package is **not published to npm**
(`npm view fast-jev-compaction` returns 404) and carries no `prepare` script, so
its TypeScript sources cannot be installed as a dependency — a `github:` install
would fetch sources with no build output. The algorithm is therefore ported
rather than depended on.

### What was ported

| Upstream source | This package |
| --- | --- |
| `src/types.ts`, `src/request.ts`, `src/client.ts` | `src/jev/protocol.ts` |
| `src/types.ts` (token estimator), `src/compact.ts` (truncation) | `src/jev/tokens.ts` |
| `src/state.ts`, `src/compact.ts` | `src/jev/plan.ts` |
| the hook's transcript→state projection and its output template | `src/surface.ts` |

Ported unchanged in behavior: the token estimator's word/digit/symbol pricing;
the tool-call pairing by `tool_use_id` and the pinned head/tail rule; the two
`noul` questions per candidate call (`keepCall`, `keepResult`); the
`keepThreshold` decision order (keep → drop result → drop call); the staged
state fitting and its stage order; the request batching arithmetic; every
default (`keepThreshold` 0.5, `preserveRecentMessages` 6, `maxStateTokens`
25000, `maxRequestTokens` 30000, `truncateHeadChars` 300, `reductionRatio`
0.25, model `jev-latest`); and the fall back to the built-in summary when the
reduction is too small, a credential is missing, or the request fails.

### What changed

- **Host, not hook.** Upstream is a Claude Code plugin driven by hook payloads.
  This package replaces the `summarize()` hook of the DeepSeek Harness
  `BasicCompactionEngine`, so DSH's own trigger policy, retention, range
  selection, locking, event bracket, and durability are inherited unchanged
  instead of being reimplemented.
- **Type names and field spelling.** Transcript types are camel-cased for DSH
  (`toolUses`, `toolResults`, `toolCallId`). The state sent to Jev deliberately
  keeps upstream's wire spelling (`i`, `role`, `text`, `tool_calls`, `id`,
  `tool`, `input`, `result`) because it is a request body, not an internal type.
- **The `noul` question variant only.** Upstream also models a `choice` question
  variant. This port carries only `noul`, which is what its plan emits, and
  drops the rest as unused.
- **Checkpoint template.** The rendered summary is a DSH checkpoint: one text
  block per retained message with `--- user ---` / `--- assistant ---` markers,
  verbatim tool inputs and retained results, and a leading ledger line that
  names the decision counts and the fit stage. Upstream's Claude Code
  transcript template is not reused.
- **Short results are kept whole.** Upstream truncates a released result to
  `truncateHeadChars` unconditionally. This port leaves a result shorter than
  the head cap plus its note intact, because truncating it would lose content
  while saving nothing; the ledger still counts it as released.
- **One request in flight per batch, all batches in parallel.** Upstream asks
  its batches sequentially; this port issues the batch requests concurrently and
  merges their answers, which shortens compaction latency at the same token
  cost.
- **No `choice`-question parsing, no hook loader, no `dist` build.** Loading,
  `.claude-plugin/`, and the Claude Code hook entry points are not ported.

## Built on DeepSeek Harness packages

`JevCompactionEngine` extends `BasicCompactionEngine` from
`@deepseek-ai/dsh-compaction-basic`, and is composed as the `ctx.compaction`
service defined by `@deepseek-ai/dsh-compaction`. Those packages are consumed as
dependencies under their own licenses; no DeepSeek Harness source is copied or
modified by this package.

